  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "Which of the following are vector fields on ${R}^{2}?$\n\n- $A(x,y)=\u27e8\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(x-y),\\operatorname{ln}\u2061(x+y)\u27e9$\n- $B(x,y,z)=\u27e8-2x,3{y}^{3},{z}^{5}\u27e9$\n- $C(t)=\u27e82-{t}^{2},{e}^{t-1}\u27e9$",
  "worked_solution": "Suppose that $S$ is a subset of ${R}^{2}.$ A vector field on ${R}^{2}$ is a function $F$ that maps every point $(x,y)$ in $S$ to a vector $F(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each function.\n\n- The function $A(x,y)$ is a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It maps every point in the set $S,$ given by\n\n$$\nS={(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,|\\,x+y>0}\n$$\n\nto a vector in ${R}^{2}.$\n\n- The function $B(x,y,z)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$ It is a vector field on ${R}^{3}.$\n\n- The function $C(t)$ is *not* a vector field on ${R}^{2}.$  It assigns points in $R$ (not ${R}^{2}$) to vectors in ${R}^{2}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
